# Reading 1 (book, "Dynamic sources and the lexical fallback"; Codex, 2026-09-28 and 2026-10-04): a free name of a
# returned method takes the nearest binding of its callers first; the node's copied lexical context is only the
# fallback.  add5 copied n 5 at makeAdder's return.  The root, with its own n 100, calls add5(1): 101.  go, with its
# formal n 7: 8.  plain has no n of its own and only hands the name on: called above the root's n, where no caller
# has one, the copy, 6; called below it, the root's 100 reaches add5 through plain: 101.  Success is 7; otherwise 64
# plus a bit per failed reading.
fn: makeAdder (int: n) fn: (int: x) int
    fn: addN (int: x) int
        return: n + x
    return: addN

add5: makeAdder 5
fn: go (int: n) int
    return: add5: 1
fn: plain () int
    return: add5: 1
int: c (plain())
int: n 100
int: a (add5: 1)
int: b (go: 7)
int: d (plain())
int: bad 0
if: a != 101
    bad: bad + 1
if: b != 8
    bad: bad + 2
if: c != 6
    bad: bad + 4
if: d != 101
    bad: bad + 8
int: ok 7
if: bad != 0
    ok: 64 + bad
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
